#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Open a terminal or an editor for one session: kitty, then tmux, then a printed command.

Usage:
    sh2pil-open pi <cwd> <session> [--fork] [--name TITLE] [--force] [--label LABEL]
            [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open opencode <cwd> <session> [--label LABEL] [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open claude <cwd> <session> [--fork] [--name TITLE] [--label LABEL]
            [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open codex <cwd> <session> [--fork] [--name TITLE] [--label LABEL]
            [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open new <cwd> [--label LABEL] [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open opencode-new <cwd> [--label LABEL] [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open claude-new <cwd> [--label LABEL] [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open codex-new <cwd> [--label LABEL] [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open shell <cwd> [--label LABEL] [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open lazygit <cwd> [--label LABEL] [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open yazi <cwd> [--label LABEL] [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open editor-dir <cwd> [--editor COMMAND] [--label LABEL] [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open editor <transcript> [--editor PATH] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open switch <cwd> <session> [--label LABEL] [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open attach <cwd> <session> [--label LABEL] [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open focus <session> [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open retitle <session> [--name NAME] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open live [--json] [--sessions-dir DIR]
    sh2pil-open state [--json] [--live-dir DIR]
    sh2pil-open state-remote <ssh-destination> [--json]
    sh2pil-open zmx-list [--json]
    sh2pil-open zmx-connect <ssh-destination> [--check] [--no-wait]
    sh2pil-open zmx-remote-list <ssh-destination> [--json]
    sh2pil-open zmx-remote-projects <ssh-destination> [--json]
    sh2pil-open zmx-switch <name> [--server SSH-DEST] [--cwd DIR] [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open zmx-remote-new <ssh-destination> <cwd> [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open zmx-history <name> [--lines N]
    sh2pil-open zmx-kill <name> [--server SSH-DEST]
    sh2pil-open zmx-copy <name>
    sh2pil-open session-remote-list <ssh-destination> [--harness SET|all] [--live] [--json]
    sh2pil-open session-remote-show <ssh-destination> <session> [--harness STORE]
            [--file PATH] [--tail N]
    sh2pil-open session-remote-open <ssh-destination> <cwd> [<session>] [--harness STORE]
    sh2pil-open session-remote-delete <ssh-destination> <session> [--harness STORE] [--force]
    sh2pil-open session-remote-prune <ssh-destination> --older-than AGE [--harness SET|all]
            [--zmx] [--yes] [--json]
            [--fork] [--name TITLE] [--label LABEL] [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open session-remote-copy <ssh-destination> <session> [--harness STORE] [--cwd DIR]
    sh2pil-open tool-remote <ssh-destination> <cwd> <lazygit|yazi> [--label LABEL]
            [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open shell-remote <ssh-destination> <cwd> [--label LABEL] [--place WHERE]
            [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open editor-remote <ssh-destination> <cwd> [--editor NAME] [--label LABEL]
            [--place WHERE] [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open harnesses-remote <ssh-destination> [--json]
    sh2pil-open paste-remote <ssh-destination> [--emit-cmd]
    sh2pil-open paste-window [--window ID]
    sh2pil-open window-target [--window ID] [--json]
    sh2pil-open window-session [--window ID] [--harness STORE] [--json]
    sh2pil-open window-menu [--window ID] [--emit-cmd]

`--place` says where an action runs: current (in this terminal pane), tab, window (its own OS
window), or pane (a new split beside the one the picker is in).  Both pickers take that from
one setting the reader cycles, so opening and forking behave alike.  tmux maps tab and window
to a new window and pane to a split.

`new` starts a fresh pi session in a directory, reusing no transcript: it is the verb for
starting work in the project a picked session lives in.  A resume and a fork both carry a
transcript forward, so neither of them can do this.  No label is set for it by default,
because pi generates the session name and writes it into the window title itself.

Four stores answer these verbs.  `pi` resumes or forks a Pi session, `opencode` resumes an
OpenCode one, `claude` resumes or forks a Claude Code one, which takes `--resume <id>` and
`--resume <id> --fork-session`, and `codex` resumes or forks a Codex one, which takes
`codex resume <id>` and `codex fork <id>`.  `claude-new`, `codex-new`, and `opencode-new`
start a chat in a directory, as `new` does for pi.  Only pi keeps a process-to-session
record, so only `switch` can find the terminal that already runs a chat; a Claude Code or
Codex resume opens a terminal and never asks about a second writer.

`switch` is what the picker uses for a session: when a pi process already owns it, the
terminal that runs it comes forward instead of a second pi joining the transcript; when
nothing runs, it resumes.  A chat that runs in a zmx session with no terminal attached is
neither: `switch` attaches to that session, because a resume would add a second writer to
the transcript.  `attach` does only that half.  `focus` reports where the session runs and
reports nothing to do (exit 1 nothing running, 2 running on a host this cannot reach).

`sh2pil` calls this for its action keys, so the same actions work from the picker,
from a plain shell, and from tmux.  The environment is detected in this order:

    tmux    $TMUX is set -- the pane is the medium the caller is working in, so the new
            terminal belongs there even when the pane sits inside kitty
    kitty   a working `kitten @ ls` -- inside kitty the controlling terminal answers,
            so no KITTY_* variables are needed, which is what a key-binding child has.
            Outside kitty, the newest socket in /tmp that answers is used instead.
    plain   print the command on stdout, for the caller to run

Nothing fails silently: a missing socket, a dead directory, or a refused launch prints
one line, and an interactive caller waits for a key before the message disappears.

Tool commands run through an interactive login shell (see SHELL below), because a kitty
child gets a PATH that holds neither `pi` nor `lazygit` nor the user's tools.  Every path
travels as its own argv element, so a project directory with spaces stays intact.  A
command that runs on another host is run in that host's own login shell, which is not
necessarily this one: the shell is chosen there, so a host with bash and no zsh is read
rather than refused.

`editor` opens no terminal: it hands the current one to `sh2pil-last --full --nvim`, the
same view as the f4 binding, so the picker's overlay becomes the reader and `q` returns
to the window.

Live sessions: two pi processes on one transcript interleave writes, because a transcript
is append-only and pi keeps no lock. The pib-live Pi extension writes a runtime record
containing the transcript, pid, and process start time, and the state of the chat: which
tool it is running, whether it is waiting on a person, or whether it has settled. `sh2pil-open
live` validates the record against the running Pi process; it never infers a session from its
working directory or recent transcript writes. A state is believed only while its clock is
fresh: a working state whose clock has stopped is reported as unknown, because a wedged
process must not be shown as busy. A record written by an older extension carries no state,
and then the record says only that the chat is live. Older processes without the extension
cannot be identified; resuming in a directory with one of those processes asks for
confirmation.

`sh2pil-open state` reads what every registered chat is doing and nothing else. It is the cheap
half of `live`: no process check, no window scan, and no transcript read, so a picker can ask
it often and follow a state on a clock. It validates nothing, and the caller keeps its answer
beside the one `live` gave, which is the read that decides whether a chat is live at all.

zmx sessions: the `zmx` key in the shared config (~/.config/sh2pil/config.yaml) runs a new,
resumed, or forked Pi chat inside a zmx session of its own, so the chat outlives the window
it was started in.  Every launch takes a fresh name, because `zmx attach` upserts and a name
that is already taken would join the running session instead.  The session is labelled as it
is created: `pi=<session id>` and `project=<directory name>`.  zmx allows only letters,
digits, and `-_.` in a label value, which is why the project is a name and not a path.

A zmx session runs its process in a session of its own (a double fork and setsid), so the
window that shows it is nowhere in pi's process ancestry, and the ancestry match that finds
every other terminal cannot find this one.  The `pi=` label is then the only link: `focus`,
`switch`, and `retitle` ask zmx which window the session is attached to.  A shell, lazygit,
yazi, or an editor gets no session, because only a chat is worth keeping alive across
terminals.

Two details follow from that. A chat opened in the picker's own terminal keeps its zmx
client there until the user detaches; the zmx session then keeps running and can be attached
again later. A `current` placement therefore stays in the current terminal. And no title is
pinned for a chat: pi names its own window `π - <session name> - <project>`, and kitty's
--title/--tab-title would freeze the label a launch passes, which is how a resumed chat kept
showing a short session id.

The picker's zmx list comes from here too.  `zmx-list` prints one row per session: the name
zmx knows, the chat its `pi=` label points at, the directory it runs in, and how many clients
are attached.  `zmx-history` prints the end of a session's own scrollback, which is what the
picker previews for a session that carries no chat.  `zmx-switch` is `switch` for one of
those rows: it brings the window that shows the session forward, and attaches a new client
when nothing shows it.  `zmx-kill` ends a session and everything it runs, and `zmx-copy`
prints the command that attaches to it.

Remote sessions: the hosts the picker knows are asked for their own sessions, and those rows
stand beside the local ones with the host in a column of its own.  `session-remote-list` runs
`sh2pil-sessions list --json` on the host, so a remote row carries the same fields a local one does.
`session-remote-show` renders a remote transcript with the host's own `sh2pil-last`, the reader
behind the f4 binding, straight from the path that host reported, so the preview pane shows
remote text the way it shows local text.  `session-remote-file` prints that transcript's own
bytes instead of a rendering, because the reader behind the f3 and f4 bindings runs in the
window the reader is in, which is this machine: the bytes travel and this machine's own
`sh2pil-last` or `sh2pil-sessions show` renders them, so a host needs nothing installed for the read and one
version of the renderer owns the view.
`window-session` is the read those bindings start with: it names the host one kitty window
runs on, and the session of that host that the window runs, from the window's own ssh process,
the one command that ssh runs there, and its title.  That command is where the store is read
too: a chat on a host is opened by one ssh command that runs the tool which starts it, so a
Claude Code or a Codex window on a host is read as that store rather than as Pi, and a chat
that resumes a session is named by the id its own command line carries.
`session-remote-open` opens an SSH terminal that resumes, forks, or starts a
chat in the remote project: this picker cannot switch to a window on another host, so every
remote action opens a new terminal, and the remote pi titles it over the SSH pty.  `session-remote-copy` prints the same command for the clipboard.  
`session-remote-delete` removes a transcript on the host itself, because the store is the
host's and its own sh2pil-sessions is what resolves the id there; the rest of the remote store is left
alone, so renaming and editing a remote transcript stay with the host.
`tool-remote` runs one directory tool on the host for the same reason: yazi and lazygit act
on the files they show, and a host's files are on that host.  lazygit is given the project
directory and finds the repository that holds it itself, because a path this machine cannot
see cannot be resolved here.  The host is asked once, before a terminal is opened, whether
the directory is there and the tool is installed, because a terminal closes at once when the
tool inside it cannot start; a host that cannot be reached is not refused by that question,
and its own terminal carries the reason.
`shell-remote` opens the host's own login shell in the host's own directory, interactive and
with no command, which is what a shell opened here is.  Only the directory is checked, and
no zmx session is made for it: only a chat is worth keeping alive across terminals, and a
zmx session is tied to the chat it runs by the `pi=` label it carries.
`editor-remote` opens one directory in the host's own editor, which is its `$EDITOR` when it
has one and nvim otherwise: an editor is a machine's own tool, with its own plugins and its
own clipboard, so no path from this machine travels with it.  The editor the host would use
is the one the check looks for.

An image on this machine's clipboard reaches a chat on another host through `paste-remote`
and `paste-window`, which the `cmd+shift+v` binding calls.  A chat reads the clipboard of the
machine it runs on, so a chat on a host has no clipboard to read: over SSH, Pi, Claude Code,
and Codex all find nothing there.  The image is therefore written into a file on the host and
the file's path is typed into the window that runs the chat, because every store takes a path
-- Pi inserts one itself when it pastes.  The path is one file in `$TMPDIR/sh2pil-paste` on the
host, mode 700 and pruned after a day, and nothing else there is touched.

The host is read from the window's own processes, never named by the caller, so one key works
on a row of every host the picker knows: the `ssh` command line kitty reports for the window
carries the destination the session was opened with.  A window whose session runs on this
machine gets the path of a temporary file here instead, which is what Pi's own `ctrl+v`
inserts.  With no image on the clipboard the terminal's own paste runs, so the key stays a
paste key in every case.

`window-target` and `window-menu` answer the `cmd+.` binding, which opens the picker's
next-step menu for the window the reader is in.  The window itself says which host and which
project the menu applies to: the `ssh` destination comes from its own processes, the same
read `paste-window` uses, and the directory comes from the zmx session the window holds a
client for, from the chat the window runs, or from kitty's own answer for a local window.
Only a host the picker already reads is accepted -- an `ssh` to a destination outside
`zmx_servers` is refused rather than guessed at -- and a window none of those rules places is
refused too, in one line the reader can act on.  `window-menu` then hands the resolved
target to `sh2pil --menu`, which draws the same menu the dot key draws inside the picker.

A host is not required to run these dotfiles.  Each read looks for the helper in PATH and then
in ~/.local/bin, and when the host has neither, the helper's code travels to it over the
connection instead: `python3 -` runs the script that arrives on standard input, so nothing is
installed and nothing is written on the host.  Both ends then run one version of the code.
A host that has a copy of its own is read through that copy, because it matches the host; when
that copy is too old to answer the read, the same read is made with the code this picker
ships, which reads the same store on that host.  So a machine whose dotfiles are one update
behind still shows its rows here instead of an error about a store its sh2pil-sessions never heard of.
The one thing such a host cannot say is which of its sessions its own pi processes run, since
only a host with the whole toolchain has `sh2pil-open` to answer that: its rows carry no live
mark.  A path in `--file` keeps a Pi read to one transcript instead of a scan of the store.

Switching to a running session needs one more step: finding the terminal that holds it.
Each medium is asked for its own list (kitty `ls`, tmux `list-panes`), the process ids it
reports are matched against the pi process, and the match is focused with that medium's
own command.  Every other part of this helper and both pickers key on the session id and
the process id, so a terminal is never assumed and a new one is one small adapter.
"""

